
  Next: how the module is loaded and tested. This repo has two incompatible conventions — [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m uses CommonJS
  ([38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m), while [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain browser script with no module system at all (loaded via [38;5;153m<script src>[39m,
  functions as globals). A preferences module has to be loadable by both the page and a test runner, so this has to be
  settled now.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the browser code adopts a module system, and therefore whether the prefs module can be imported by
  tests directly.

  - [1mES modules ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m)[0m — [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m exports get/set/reset; [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports it. Native in every current
    browser, no bundler. Tests import the same file. Requires serving over [38;5;153mhttp://[39m rather than opening the file directly
    (ES modules are blocked on [38;5;153mfile://[39m), and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes a module.
  - [1mCommonJS, matching [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[0m — consistent with the existing Node code and trivially testable, but browsers can't
    load [38;5;153mrequire()[39m natively, so the page needs a bundler or a duplicated build. New infrastructure for a two-file app.
  - [1mPlain global script, matching [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today[0m — [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m defines a global, zero setup, page keeps working from [38;5;153mfile://[39m.
    But testing means loading the script into a fake global and there's no import boundary, so it's the least isolated
    of the three.

  Cheap to change later: nothing here, really — module format propagates into every file that touches prefs, so
  switching means editing all of them.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the one option that gives the page and the tests the same file with no build step,
  and the only cost is running a local static server instead of double-clicking the HTML.
